of what enterprise-readiness

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.200
means, let's talk
about certain building

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.144
blocks of Kubernetes Engine.

00:06:54.144 --> 00:06:55.560
These building
blocks will be used

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.430
to explain other
topics in the section

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:03.090
so it will be useful over
the next 40 minutes, as well.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.200
We all know about
Virtual Private Cloud.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.120
This is the building
block of GCP networking.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.940
A Virtual Private
Cloud in GCP provides

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.360
a global, fully meshed,
and a private IP network.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.750
This is, in fact, the
customers' networks

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.500
lies in the GCP network.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.480
Within a VPC, we have
subnetworks which provides

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.780
regional boundaries with a VPC.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:30.900
And they give a
location connotation.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:33.420
These two are the fundamental
building blocks of GCP,

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.930
whether you're doing GCP,
or GKE, or GCE, or anything

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:38.860
of that sort.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.400
Kubernetes Engine adds a
few more networking elements

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.032
to the mix.

00:07:42.032 --> 00:07:42.990
Let's talk about those.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.010
First among them is the
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:50.320
A cluster consists of
nodes, parts, and services.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.770
A cluster also has
a cluster master

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.950
which is the brains behind
controlling the cluster.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:57.900
It provides the control
plane functionality

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.290
to manage the cluster.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:02.850
A VPC can have
multiple clusters.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.140
Nodes are VM instances
on which pods are run.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:11.310
The nodes compute networking
and storage resources

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.840
to the different pods.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:15.090
Depending upon the pod's
resource constraints,

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.770
you can have nodes
of different sizes.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:22.565
The node gets an IP and
it's routable by default.

00:08:22.565 --> 00:08:25.590
Pods, think of them as tiny
task-specific processes

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.320
that are running on
top of your nodes.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.290
A pod gets an IP
automatically and is also

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:35.730
routable by default. A pod can
run one container or multiple

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.770
containers, such as
a docker container.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.686
And when there are
multiple containers running

00:08:39.686 --> 00:08:43.860
on top of a pod, all of them
are managed as a single entity,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.410
sharing the
resources of the pod.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.890
Services.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.410
A service is something that
is long-lived and provides

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.610
a stable and a fixed end point
to represent all your pods.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.190
A pod, on the other
hand, is ephemeral.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.420
It can come and go because
of failures or scalability.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:06.520
So pods are ephemeral.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.400
Services are long-lived.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.420
Services basically provide an
abstraction for all your pods.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.880
A good example for services
is a load balancer.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:20.320
All this come together in
a flat networking model

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.550
where VPC is the underlying
glue that provides

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.270
a fully meshed network.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.070
And in this fully
meshed network,

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.110
all the nodes can
talk to each other.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:31.000
All the parts can
talk to each other.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:35.680
And all the nodes and parts can
talk to each other, by default.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.420
The benefits of this
model become more obvious

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.010
in the next few sections.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:43.770
Deployment simplicity.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:49.080
So one size doesn't fit all
and this is especially true

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.120
for enterprise networking
that has evolved over time.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.030
So Kubernetes Engine has an
interesting challenge at hand.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.320
For deployment
simplicity, it needs

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.467
to ensure that it's
providing the best

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:02.550
practices in networking
and enterprise networking,

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:03.420
especially.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.070
And at the same
time, integrate well

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:09.277
with an enterprises' on-prem,
as well as Cloud presence.

00:10:09.277 --> 00:10:11.360
So let's talk about what
are the different aspects

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.690
within deployment simplicity.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:13.650
One is control.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.200
First and foremost, customers
do not like surprises.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:19.390
They want tight control
over all their resources.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.100
One way Kubernetes
Engine delivers

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.160
this is by having
distinct ranges

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.940
for the different resources.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:29.940
So if corporate has a
policy, manage a policy

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.190
where they see the notion
not talk to each other,

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.060
but the pods should be able
to talk to each other--

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.280
by having distinct ranges for
nodes, pods, and services,

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.580
it makes it a more
intuitive model for you

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.040
to apply such a policy.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.470
You can use a firewall
policy to block the nodes

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.120
and that pods, by default,
are talking to each other.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.050
This is a much simpler model
to implement in your network.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.960
Control is also
extended in the form

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.520
of provisioning flexibility.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.330
If you're an
enterprise customer who

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.360
has a very intricate
deployment scheme,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.500
you can either let us
decide the addresses for you

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.010
these address engine for you.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:12.300
Or you also have the
user-defined option.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.060
And the user-defined option
is very critical for, again,

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.760
for the customers who have
a very intricate scheme.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.630
Just as how you are
define an IP in a VPC

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.180
and you map that to a
region through subnetworks,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.580
you can create
custom subnetworks

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.700
and map the nodes,
pods, and services

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.460
in a particular region.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:35.130
This gives you very
fine-grained control.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.740
This makes the
cluster size fungible.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.710
And also solves the
bin packing problem.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:42.400
Forgot to animate this.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.990
So in this example, as you can
see, since these ranges are

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.760
different, the
nodes-- you can apply

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.600
a firewall policy or
a security policy,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.610
which stops the node from
talking to each other,

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:55.410
but pods can talk to each other.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.420
Centralized management.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.380
As more customers start
deploying Kubernetes Engine,

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.760
we have seen that
one of the most

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.260
often asked questions
from customers

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.480
is around risk management.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.700
For certain large-sized
enterprises,

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.220
IP addresses-- even
private IP addresses--

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.750
is a precious commodity.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:14.880
They want to ensure that
the scheme that they land

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:18.600
on for addressing, makes
sense, works for them as they

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:20.490
scale out in future.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:24.650
So Kubernetes Engine solves
this problem for the customer

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:26.850
by understanding
what is being used.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:28.380
Where it is being used.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:29.190
What is available.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:32.430
And what is the min/max size
for the different resources.

00:12:37.224 --> 00:12:38.640
Another advantage
of this model is

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:42.960
that we track the addresses
as you grow your cluster

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.250
across your cluster.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.400
So with this tracking
and check, we

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.660
ensure that there is
no address collusion

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.677
as you grow more
resources in the cluster

00:12:50.677 --> 00:12:52.260
or you grow-- even
your clusters-- you

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.300
grow into multiple clusters.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.990
This tracking is done
dynamically and at runtime.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:01.230
And it also shows that not
only is your GKE footprint

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:03.730
not getting address
collision, but even

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.420
your GCP footprint is not
getting any address collision.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.670
So if you are building
something on GKE

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.460
and you're also using
certain elements on GCP--

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.590
let's say, a GCE
or internal load

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:20.430
balancer-- we ensure that there
is no collision across the two.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:21.510
Integrated networking.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:23.250
So when Kubernetes
was introduced,

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:26.570
there were multiple models
to implement and contain IP

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:27.780
connectivity.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:31.110
Some implemented encapsulated
overlay networks.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.060
There was network
route programming

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.200
for directing the
container traffic.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:40.380
But in both these
models, the networking

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:43.420
was bolted onto the
Cloud infrastructure,

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.230
making it less
agile and coming up

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:48.750
with a list of features
that were [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.930
With the launch of
VPC native clusters,

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:53.580
clusters and their
different resources

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.255
are now integrated within
the Google infrastructure.

00:13:57.255 --> 00:13:59.130
All the clusters and
the different resources,

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:02.370
again, are first class
citizens within the VPC.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:06.780
Now what that means is features
like shared VPC, VPC peering,

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:10.110
access to Google services,
all are available by default

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:14.370
to your GKE applications
running on a Kubernetes Engine.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:18.180
As an example, if
you're using pods

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.270
and you are accessing
these services--

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.660
Cloud Storage,
Cloud with table--

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.250
over a public IP,
with VPC native,

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:27.870
you can now access
these services

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.420
over the pods
private IP, as well.

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:33.260
It's seamless.

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:34.710
Lastly, hybrid support.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.400
No Kubernetes
Engine deployment is

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:40.000
complete without providing
support for hybrid networking.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.600
We want to ensure that for
easy onboarding of Kubernetes

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:46.930
Engine, the mechanisms
that exist today to make

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:49.060
on-prem pop to Cloud,
work seamlessly

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:50.900
for Kubernetes Engine, as well.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.480
So with that in mind,
Kubernetes Engine clusters

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:55.510
are transparent to on-prem.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:57.250
We have the concept
of Cloud routers

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.370
which facilitates the
communication between on-prem

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.800
and GKE clusters seamlessly.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.370
You can use the existing
VPN mechanism, whether that

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:08.560
is Cloud VPN or dedicated
interconnect or partner

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:11.460
interconnect, and
connect from your on-prem

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:13.300
to the Kubernetes clusters.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.310
You can also have
very tight control

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:17.540
over what gets to talk to whom.

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.200
So if you want to ensure
that from on-prem,

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:21.910
only certain
applications or pods

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.260
are available to
communicate with, again,

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:27.580
from the help of Cloud
router and using routing,

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:31.090
you can advertise specific
subnets between the on-prem

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:32.500
and the Cloud.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:34.160
With that, I'll hand
it over to Manjot.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:34.660
Thank you.

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:44.420
MANJOT PAHWA: Thank
you so much, Mahesh.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:47.770
Now I will talk about security.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:49.540
You all have a tough job.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.480
Security is one of those
things which is never enough.

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:57.750
And we at GKE, believe in having
both belts and suspenders.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.920
And now I will
talk about how GKE

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:03.700
tries to ensure that all
your surface areas of attack

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:05.820
are covered.

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:08.530
We at GKE, believe in a
layered security model

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:13.630
where we cover every aspect and
every layer of your deployment

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:15.130
that should be secure.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:19.150
Broadly dividing it into config
time and runtime security,

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.670
where config time
consists of separation

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:25.330
of administrative control
for your deployments.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.910
And runtime consists
of private deployments

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:29.860
and network security.

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:32.350
Now, separation of
administrative control,

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:35.170
what it enables you
to do is, typically,

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:38.440
you would have an organization
with an infrastructure

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:40.150
and a platform team,
which would want

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:43.330
to control your critical
network resources

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:47.020
and still have your
app or your front end

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:51.740
teams be able to create and
manage their GKE clusters.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:56.260
And runtime security-- private
deployment enables you to get

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:59.920
network segmentation where
your back end services--

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.440
which shouldn't be
exposed on the internet--

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:03.660
are not.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:07.089
And network security
dives a layer

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.910
deeper from private
deployments and drills

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:11.920
down within your
cluster to enable

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:15.319
you to control your
interservice communication.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:18.861
Shared VPC.

00:17:18.861 --> 00:17:20.319
I will pick up the
first one, which

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:23.329
was a separation of
administrative controls.

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:27.970
So shared VPC enables you to
map your organizational topology

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.550
onto your critical
network resources.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:34.370
And how does it actually
allow you to do that?

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:36.220
So Kubernetes solved
the problem of enabling

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.520
you to write your
infrastructure as code,

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.980
but we need to still reconcile
your operational security

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:43.600
models with that.

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:49.150
And what shared VPC does is it
enables your critical network

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.780
resources-- such as firewalls
and other such resources--

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:56.520
to be controlled by a handful
of network and security admins.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.690
And your project or
your cluster admins

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:02.350
still have the ability to
be able to create clusters

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:05.650
within a shared
network and not have

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.080
to deal with things like
network and security

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:09.760
critical resources.

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:12.610
Developers should be able
to code, test, and deploy

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.220
their applications without
having to deal with questions

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.880
of connectivity or
firewalls and shared

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:22.010
VPC enables you to do that.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:25.030
So talking about, what
are the benefits that

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:27.035
shared VPC actually gives you--

00:18:27.035 --> 00:18:28.660
first thing that I've
already mentioned

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:34.060
is the separation of your
security and your project admin

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:35.710
roles.

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:39.020
So typically, in a deployment
or an organization,

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:40.750
you will have your
infrastructure teams

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:42.791
and your platform teams
controlling those network

00:18:42.791 --> 00:18:43.590
resources.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:46.510
And have your project
teams or application teams,

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:49.180
being able to create and deploy
clusters in the shared subnets,

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:53.050
without having the ability
to change those resources.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:56.290
And that gives you
separation of rules.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:59.800
Second, sharing of
common resources.

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:03.040
Isolation is great, but in
deployments, you certainly

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.230
have some resources that
you would like to share

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:07.180
across all those clusters.

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:09.820
And shared VPC enables
you to still do

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:13.980
that and have that
isolation that you require.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:17.430
Third, isolation of data
and support for multi-tenant

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.320
workloads.

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.780
By dividing your
workloads and your teams

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:25.770
into separate projects that
you can attach to your larger

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:29.130
project or your
organizational host project,

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:32.940
you get isolation of
data so as to prevent

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:38.850
deliberate or inadvertent
attacks that you might get.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:42.240
And fourth, more
granular billing.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:45.930
We often hear enterprises
wanting more granular billing

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:46.980
on a team basis.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:50.910
And by dividing your
organization into those teams

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:53.100
and mapping those
teams to projects

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:57.720
that work in a shared VPC model,
you get more granular billing.

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:58.850
Let's look under the hood.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:01.420
I think this is too
fuzzy [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:04.320
So you have your
organization and then

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:05.790
you have your host project.

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:08.970
Your host project consists
of your shared VPC network,

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:12.330
which consists of a few subnets
and all the critical network

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:15.270
resources that you
want to control.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:17.386
Then you have your
different apps teams.

00:20:17.386 --> 00:20:18.510
You have your apps project.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.010
You have a database,
infrastructure,

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:21.468
and many other
teams that you might

00:20:21.468 --> 00:20:23.430
have in your organization.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.640
And what you do is you map
all of them to what we call,

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:27.720
service projects.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:31.140
And you attach all those service
projects to your host project.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.350
This way, using IAM
permissions, you

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:37.410
give permissions to our
project or your team admins

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:40.110
to be able to create
and manage clusters

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:42.390
within the boundaries
of their project,

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.490
without affecting
any other team.

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.550
And you enable the network
and security admins

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.550
to be able to control
the critical network

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:54.300
resources that you don't want
the project admins affecting.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.340
And so this enables
each and every team

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:58.998
to work independently
and with agility.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:05.140
Moving on to the
second pillar and this

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:06.790
comes under runtime security--

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:09.250
private deployments.

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:12.760
Private deployments is a
very, very crucial factor

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:14.170
for a lot of enterprises.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.170
A lot of enterprises want to
ensure that their Kubernetes

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:20.110
Engine deployments are
within the secure boundary

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:22.600
of their VPC.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:25.630
Typically, you have a
multi-tiered application.

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.910
You have a few front
end apps and you have

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.620
a lot of back end services.

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:32.170
And your back end services--
which are not exposed

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:35.110
to the internet and don't
require public IPs--

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:36.400
shouldn't have them.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:39.490
And private clusters
enables you to do that.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:44.010
Private clusters enables you to
cordon off your control plane

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:47.350
and to enable
network segmentation

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:49.460
by removing public
IPs from the nodes

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:53.530
and taking your workloads
off the public internet.

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:58.150
Second of all, with some
of the lot advancements

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.030
in our peering capabilities,
the communication

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:03.850
between your nodes and
the Kubernetes Master

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:05.950
is completely private.

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:09.670
And third, using
private clusters.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:12.700
Using private clusters,
you can ensure

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:16.870
that you set your control
plane to default deny.

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:20.290
This way, only things
that you trust,

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.510
only the perimeter
that you trust

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:25.000
has access to your
GKE deployments.

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:28.060
And unauthorized access--
whether it's to your nodes

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:29.510
or to your control plane--

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:30.480
is completely blocked.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.380
Master authorized networks.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:38.560
Now, with private clusters,
you took your nodes

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:40.820
and your workloads off
the public internet,

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:42.670
but you want to
be able to manage

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:47.470
the access to your control plane
in a more fine-grain manner.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:50.470
You definitely want to
block unauthorized access,

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:52.690
but there might be
some trusted set of IPs

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.420
that you still
want to have access

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.080
to your Kubernetes Master.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:59.470
So master authorized
networks is the option

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.840
to set a white-list
on your [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.960
to have access from their
trusted set of IPs, which

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:07.600
maybe on your
premise or elsewhere

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:12.010
and can control your deployment.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:16.690
So moving on to network security
and drilling down to clusters.

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:20.690
So private deployments ensures
that your clusters are secure.

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:24.120
Network policy works
within a single cluster.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.190
Network security
with network policies

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:30.690
ensures that in a model where
you have multiple services

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.940
within your single
cluster, they are

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:36.220
secure by being segmented
from each other.

00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:39.240
So let's say, you have some
vulnerability which affected

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.160
the front end services.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:44.970
We want to ensure that
your front end services are

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:47.640
compromised, but then, they
don't affect critical services,

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.340
like billing.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:52.950
And network policies
enable you to do that.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.830
Network policies are
user-defined policies

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:58.110
that you set on your
deployment, which

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:00.210
get enforced in real-time.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:05.130
And Kubernetes Engine ensures
that the network policy

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:09.762
that you control are enforced
in real-time and to the scale

00:24:09.762 --> 00:24:11.095
that Kubernetes Engine supports.

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:19.750
Now, let me sum up all of the
things that I've mentioned

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:23.510
and give you an overview
of how we see security.

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:26.600
Broadly dividing into config
time and runtime, where

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:29.540
you get separation of
administrative control

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:32.090
so that you can map your
organizational topology

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:37.010
to your network, is using shared
VPC with granular IAM rules.

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:38.930
So that you can ensure
that your project

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:40.820
admins create
clusters, but do not

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:44.000
affect those critical
network resources.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:46.340
Runtime security consisting
of, first of all,

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:50.770
private deployments, which
Brad had spoken about earlier.

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.260
Private clusters enables you
to take off your clusters

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:57.439
from the public
internet and ensure

00:24:57.439 --> 00:24:59.480
with master authorized
networks that your control

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:03.110
plane is cordoned off, and
gives access only to the trusted

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:05.650
set of IPs that you control.

00:25:05.650 --> 00:25:10.810
Network security achieved
through the network policies,

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:14.020
which ensures that in a
microservices model where you

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:16.360
have multiple services
within your cluster,

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.520
you control exactly
which services

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:22.060
can talk to which
other services, instead

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:24.884
of the previous way of having
a network level firewall which

00:25:24.884 --> 00:25:26.050
gives you an all or nothing.

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:32.110
Now, moving on to visibility.

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:35.830
Security is obviously,
an important aspect

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:37.150
of all your deployments.

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:40.630
And having visibility into what
is going on within your network

00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:43.060
is equally important.

00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:45.910
I will show you how some
of the things that we

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:50.030
are launching at Kubernetes
Engine help you do that.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:54.220
So logging and monitoring
are the cornerstones

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.210
of your deployments
and Kubernetes Engine

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:01.960
ensures that you have enough
visibility and flexibility

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:07.870
to get information about the
traffic flow within your VPC.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:10.780
And VPC flow logs
enables you to do that.

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:14.590
VPC flow logs captures
the traffic flow,

00:26:14.590 --> 00:26:16.780
whether its within your VPC.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:19.960
Whether it's to and
from the internet.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:24.130
Or whether it's from your
on-premises to your deployment.

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:28.100
Or if you're using a
host of Google services.

00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:31.930
So VPC flow logs ensures that
it captures all the traffic

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:34.930
flows that are there within
your deployment, which

00:26:34.930 --> 00:26:39.590
you might want to look
into for several reasons.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:44.000
VPC flow logs also had to
optimize your network usage

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:47.030
and egress, enabling
you to better budget

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:50.150
for your deployments.

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:53.270
Now VPC flow logs
is a raw form of log

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:59.720
that you can use to
visualize with your choice

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:02.000
of visualization engine.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:05.240
And we have integration
with a host of partners,

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:09.000
or you can use Stackdriver.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:12.420
And VPC flow logs
also enables you

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:16.290
to get network forensics
and security analysis,

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:20.700
to dive deeper into any attacks
that you might have experience

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:24.210
or within your deployment.

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:28.950
And lastly, it also gives you
a real-time security analysis

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:31.960
with a five-second time.

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:34.690
VPC flow logs is also
flexible in the sense

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:37.030
that you can export
that information

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:41.890
in a very secure manner to
BigQuery or to Stackdriver

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.320
Logging-- as I
already mentioned.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:48.580
Moreover, you might
have platforms

00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:51.580
that you prefer to visualize
that log information.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:54.070
And you can use Cloud
Pub/Sub to actually

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:57.220
export that information out and
visualize it the way you want.

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:03.140
This is a typical way in which
you can visualize information

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:07.125
from VPC flow logs,
where it captures

00:28:07.125 --> 00:28:12.650
the flows that are there between
your nodes and your pods.

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:16.340
And you can see here, the
overall traffic that is flowing

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:17.450
through your deployment.

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:22.190
You can see things like is it
coming from central, or Europe,

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:24.860
or which zone or
region is coming from?

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.447
And you can also visualize
the busiest machines

00:28:27.447 --> 00:28:28.280
that you might have.

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:34.086
Now, I will call
upon Bowei to give

00:28:34.086 --> 00:28:35.930
a cool demo of some
of the features

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:39.230
that we just spoke about.

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:41.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:44.860
BOWEI DU: So hi.

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:45.850
Thanks, Manjot.

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.820
In this demo, we'll dig into how
a single application spread up

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:50.680
across multiple teams
and responsibilities

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.330
will map to shared VPC.

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:54.550
And really see how shared
VPC and private clusters

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:57.040
will work in practice
to secure your apps.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:29:00.310
So suppose you have a
multi-tier application that

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:01.540
spans multiple teams.

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:03.760
In this example, you
have an apps team

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:06.760
responsible for the front
end, exposing the application

00:29:06.760 --> 00:29:08.050
service to the internet.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:10.720
You have an infra-team
who manages the back end.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.590
Now for development agility
and finer-grained resource

00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:16.600
accounting, you
would like each team

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:19.630
to be responsible for
their Kubernetes cluster,

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.820
but you have a net
ops team that's

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:25.180
responsible for the overall
security and network resource

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:26.080
management.

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:27.700
So the question
is, how would you

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:32.190
map this to Google Cloud
and Kubernetes Engine?

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:35.340
So first, we start
with the host project--

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:38.720
the VPC network, which is
owned by the net ops team.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.950
They're responsible
for IP assignments.

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:43.330
Firewall, generally speaking.

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:44.714
Access and resource allocation.

00:29:44.714 --> 00:29:46.630
And they have full control
over the networking

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:49.250
aspects of the host project.

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:52.870
Next, you have the apps team
and their service project.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:56.060
And they will be managing
the front end application.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.250
Also, they will be
managing your users

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:00.840
coming in from the internet
hitting the application.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:03.410
Finally, you have
the infra-team.

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:06.320
And now, something to notice
is that this infra-team also

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:08.060
has an additional requirement.

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:10.520
They want to keep the nodes
in their Kubernetes cluster

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:12.980
completely private,
but also want

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:16.140
to be able to be accessible
from the application.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:19.040
So let's dive in and see
what this looks like.

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:19.710
And whoops.

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:35.690
So we have this
set up from before.

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:38.610
So let's dive in and
see what the shared VPC

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:42.940
setup looks like in your
Google Cloud Project.

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:48.660
So as mentioned before, you
have a shared VPC host project

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:53.460
that will be owned
by the network admin.

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:56.790
In addition, you have
attached service projects

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:57.722
for each of the teams.

00:30:57.722 --> 00:30:59.430
So you'll see an
attached service project

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:01.860
for the infra-team, an
attached service project

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:02.730
for the apps team.

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:08.920
Now, let's zoom in
on the apps team

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:11.330
and what their
application looks like.

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:14.290
So for the apps team, they're
using Kubernetes Engine.

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:16.730
And let's take a look
at our application.

00:31:16.730 --> 00:31:19.030
It's a pod running
on the cluster.

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:21.790
And the services is a
load balancer service,

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:26.150
but first, we need to open up
the firewall for this service.

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:28.400
When you're running in a
shared VPC environment,

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:29.930
you can create
these service, you

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:32.240
can expose them
as load balancer--

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:34.310
using standard Kubernetes
primitives-- but it's

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:37.700
up to the net ops team to
actually expose the firewall.

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:39.500
So let's do that.

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:46.840
So this will be done
by the net ops team.

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:49.785
They will receive a request say,
hey, I'm creating a service.

00:31:49.785 --> 00:31:51.160
I want to put it
on the internet.

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:52.701
And it will be up
in the net ops team

00:31:52.701 --> 00:31:55.600
to expose the service, which
is these two firewalls.

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:58.180
That then will expose
the Kubernetes service

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:01.530
to the internet users.

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:04.320
So we open the
firewall, what next?

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:08.070
Let's zoom in on the infra-team.

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:10.120
So the infra-team is
managing a back end,

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:14.280
serving database and other data.

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:16.770
Now, first of all,
it's a private cluster

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:21.540
so actually, you can't access
it directly from the internet

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:23.320
as you can with
a normal cluster.

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:24.820
And you will have
to use the bastion

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:25.944
host to access the cluster.

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:35.590
In the infra-side, you have an
application running as a pod.

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:37.120
And you will also
need to open up

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:40.970
a firewall between
the apps and the DB.

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:44.380
They're part of the same
VPC so they're connectable,

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:49.420
but you'll have to do some
work to open the firewall so

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:51.370
that these two can communicate.

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:54.310
And as you see here, the net ops
team will be in charge of this.

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:57.550
And they will open an
apps to back end firewall.

00:32:57.550 --> 00:33:03.730
Finally, let's see these
two pieces in concert.

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:07.990
So we have the application
front end on the right side.

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:10.510
We have the infrastructure
back end on the left side.

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:17.720
And here is our application.

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:19.720
So our application
is very simple.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:23.010
It says, hello next 2018.

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.890
But as you can see, it
comes in from the internet,

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:26.890
hits the app.

00:33:26.890 --> 00:33:30.090
The app then queries the
back end inside the VPC

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:33.629
in a secure way and the reply
goes out back to the user.

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:45.080
So throughout this
presentation, we've

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:46.850
seen what it means
for Kubernetes Engine

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:49.260
to be a great platform
for enterprise workloads.

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:52.890
Now I want you to take away four
things from this presentation.

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:55.250
First, is that Kubernetes
Engine gives you

00:33:55.250 --> 00:33:56.540
deployment simplicity.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:59.420
So it gives you the tools to
control, manage, and scale

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:01.940
your cluster resources
in a centralized manner.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:04.310
And your clusters will
integrate seamlessly

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:05.930
with other GCP resources.

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:08.560
Basically, everything is
connected and part of one VPC.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:10.219
And as you could
see in the demo,

00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:12.470
you have multiple teams
and they all share one VPC

00:34:12.470 --> 00:34:13.969
and you can create
applications that

00:34:13.969 --> 00:34:16.790
span multiple teams,
multiple projects,

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:19.730
but use the same network.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:23.719
Second, we get
fine-grained control

00:34:23.719 --> 00:34:25.580
and shared VPC enables
you a separation

00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:28.520
of administrative roles so
that your network resources are

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:30.530
managed by network
ops, without hampering

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:35.179
the productivity of your other
teams in creating clusters.

00:34:35.179 --> 00:34:35.989
You get security.

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:38.572
So with Kubernetes Engine, you
can make every path and surface

00:34:38.572 --> 00:34:40.449
area secure with
private clusters

00:34:40.449 --> 00:34:42.139
and master authorized networks.

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:44.540
And finally, visibility
with VPC flow

00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:47.750
logs, you get views into all
of the flows in your deployment

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:48.889
and your VPC.

00:34:48.889 --> 00:34:52.010
And the one thing I want you to
take away is that all of this

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.320
is integrated in an
unified experience for all

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:55.889
of your workloads.

00:34:55.889 --> 00:34:57.530
So thank you for
coming to our session

00:34:57.530 --> 00:34:58.970
and now we will have the Q&amp;A.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:01.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

